Conformance
Id	Clause
conformance.1	This specification defines two classes of product. A converter reads an input of JSON values [RFC8259] that a module specification describes and writes MTSV files [MTSV-DRAFT]; a converter is a generator of [MTSV-DRAFT] and MUST meet that document's requirements for generators. A data bank [CODD1970] stores the files a converter writes for each of its inputs and communicates them to another system; a data bank uses a converter. A module specification supplies what the schema rules name, and restates nothing this specification states; this specification names no source.
conformance.2	A converter conforms if, for every conformance input and its module specification, it writes the expected file, with the same sheets in the same order and the same sheet names, headers and records, and reports the same text not carried; and if it rejects every conformance input and module specification it must reject. A data bank conforms if, for every conformance input stored in parts, it communicates the expected sheets. A conformance input is a JSON Lines file [JSONL], one input value to a line.
conformance.3	The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this specification are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [BCP14] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.
conformance.4	The Rules and the Fields are normative. Each rule states one requirement, and its Id names the group it governs and the requirement's number within the group. The groups come in the order the product works, in five parts: the input, JSON: schema, what a module specification supplies and how its schema is read, and value, the input values; the transfer from the input's tree to relations: relation, the relation and column each value is written to, key, how each record is identified, and order, the order of sheets, records and columns; the output, MTSV: file, sheet, record and field, the levels of [MTSV-DRAFT]; the storage: storage, how a data bank keeps what a converter writes; and the communication: communication, how a data bank gives what it keeps to another system. The first three parts are one procedure: the input a tree of relations, the transfer its normalization, each subordinate relation taking its parent's primary key as a foreign key, and the output the relations in normal form as arrays, a communication form between systems; where the graph of nonsimple domains is not a tree, a kind referred to from several places or holding itself, a foreign key refers to the kind's one relation [CODD1970]. The array representation in normal form serves storage purposes and the communication of bulk data between systems [CODD1970], the last two parts. A rule names each source it follows in brackets, as [RFC8259]; the References list them, normative first, then informative. This specification has no optional features: where a source leaves a choice to the implementation, a rule closes it.
conformance.5	File, sheet, sheet name, header, record, field, line break, generator and parser are as defined in [MTSV-DRAFT]; column as in [CSVW]. JSON text, value, object, member, name, array, element, string and number are as in [RFC8259]; JSON Lines as in [JSONL]; JSON Pointer and reference token as in [RFC6901]; fragment as in [RFC3986]; schema, root schema, subschema, keyword, assertion, annotation, instance, instance location, primitive type and property as in [JSON-SCHEMA-07]; relation, domain, simple domain, nonsimple domain, repeating group, primary key, foreign key, subordinate relation, role name, data bank, time-varying relation, insertion, deletion, alteration, storage, communication and access path as in [CODD1970]; entity, attribute and 1:n mapping as in [CHEN1976]; record type as in [KENT1983]; fixed variable, observational unit, molten, and filter, transform, aggregate and sort as in [WICKHAM2014]. Page, line and position are the units FF, LF and HT move to [RFC20]; a run of text is a region of text that shares a common set of properties [ECMA-376-1]. A module specification is the specification of the module that reads one source for a converter.
Rules
Id	Rule
schema.1	A module specification MUST supply a schema of draft-07, valid against its metaschema [JSON-SCHEMA-07], as a JSON text of any size, any depth of nesting and any length of string [RFC8259], against which every value of its input validates.
schema.2	The root schema MUST hold a title annotation, which names the sheet of the input values (sheet.1) [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
schema.3	Every name the schema gives a sheet or a column, a title, a property's name, a key of patternProperties or dependencies, or the last reference token of a $ref, MUST be text a field can hold, holding no HT, LF, FF or CR [MTSV-DRAFT].
schema.4	A regular expression of pattern or patternProperties MUST use only the tokens schema authors should limit themselves to: individual characters, simple and complemented character classes and ranges, the quantifiers +, *, ?, {x}, {x,y} and {x,} and their lazy forms, the anchors ^ and $, and simple grouping and alternation, none taken to be anchored [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
schema.5	A $ref MUST reference a schema within the supplied schema, the one schema a converter is given [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
schema.6	A $ref's fragment MUST be a JSON Pointer in its URI fragment representation [RFC6901], of the characters a URI fragment holds [RFC3986], $id not being read (schema.15).
schema.7	A $ref MUST NOT reach again, at the same instance location through allOf, anyOf, oneOf, not, if, then, else or a dependency, a schema it is applied from: a schema run into an infinite loop against a schema has undefined behavior [JSON-SCHEMA-07], and where no parent standard decides, the more restrictive rule holds at the door.
schema.8	A module specification that breaks schema.1-7 is non-conforming, and a converter MUST reject it [RFC9413].
schema.9	A converter MUST read the schema's keywords by their kind [JSON-SCHEMA-07]: those that apply subschemas to child locations, items, additionalItems, properties, patternProperties and additionalProperties, and to the same location, allOf, anyOf, oneOf, if, then, else and dependencies, shape the relations (relation.6-15); type and required decide each value's place (relation.4, relation.5); and title names sheets (sheet.1, sheet.2).
schema.10	A converter MUST read a $ref as the schema it references, its other members ignored, its fragment percent-decoded as UTF-8 and evaluated as a JSON Pointer [RFC6901]; the schema a $ref references is a kind (relation.2) [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
schema.11	A converter MUST take a schema's subschemas from its keywords in the order [JSON-SCHEMA-07] presents them, items, additionalItems, properties, patternProperties, additionalProperties, dependencies, if, then, else, allOf, anyOf and oneOf, and within a keyword in the order the schema writes them.
schema.12	A converter MUST apply every assertion of the schema in validation (value.9) [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
schema.13	A converter MUST NOT shape a sheet from not, whose annotations are ignored, propertyNames, which applies to names, definitions, which applies to nothing by itself, or contains, whose elements items places [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
schema.14	A converter MUST NOT assert format, contentMediaType or contentEncoding, which an implementation may or may not validate [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
schema.15	A converter MUST ignore every other keyword, $id among them, as an implementation ignores the keywords it does not support [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
value.1	A converter MUST read the input values, each a JSON text in UTF-8 [RFC8259] [RFC3629], in the order its module supplies them, the order its source holds them, and MUST NOT sort them.
value.2	A converter MUST give each value its zero-based position in the whole input (key.2); an input converted in parts, in order, keeps its positions, so that the sheets each part gives, appended sheet by sheet in the file's order (order.1), are the whole input's file [MTSV-DRAFT].
value.3	A converter MUST accept every input value, of any size, any depth of nesting and any length of string, a parser being allowed to set limits that a converter does not set [RFC8259].
value.4	A converter MUST NOT ignore a byte order mark, which is not part of a JSON text and which a parser may ignore [RFC8259]: a value that begins with one is not a JSON text (value.7).
value.5	A converter MUST read the members of an object in the order written [RFC8259].
value.6	A converter MUST read a number as the text written, an implementation being allowed to limit the range and precision of numbers [RFC8259]; an integer is a number, the data model making no distinction between them [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
value.7	A converter MUST reject an input value that is not a JSON text in UTF-8 [RFC8259] [RFC3629].
value.8	A converter MUST reject an input value that holds an object whose names are not all unique, whose behavior is undefined [JSON-SCHEMA-07] and whose member a JSON Pointer cannot reference [RFC6901].
value.9	A converter MUST reject an input value that fails an assertion of its schema (schema.12) [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
value.10	A converter MUST name a rejected value by its zero-based position in the input and the JSON Pointer to the place within it [RFC6901] [RFC9413]: where an assertion fails, the deepest instance location where it fails; where names are not unique, the first object, in the order written, that repeats one; and where the value is not a JSON text, the value itself.
relation.1	A relation is a sheet [CODD1970], and a converter MUST write the input values as one relation, the sheet of the input values; where a $ref references the root schema, the root schema is a kind, and the sheet of the input values is its kind's one sheet (relation.2), one type of observational unit in one table [WICKHAM2014].
relation.2	One type of observational unit in several places is combined into one table [WICKHAM2014], and a schema a $ref references describes one type wherever it is referenced [JSON-SCHEMA-07]; so each such schema is a kind, and a converter MUST write every instance that a $ref applies to a child location, or to the same location through anyOf, oneOf, if, then, else or a dependency, to its kind's one sheet, placed by the kind's schema alone, as a child location's instances are, wherever it is referenced.
relation.3	A converter MUST read a $ref that allOf applies as adding to its instance's own schema (relation.8), not as a kind [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
relation.4	A converter MUST take the primitive types an instance location allows as those its type keyword lists, all six where it has none, narrowed by every subschema allOf applies there and by the union of the branches of anyOf and oneOf [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
relation.5	By those types, and by whether its schema requires it, a converter MUST write each value in one of three places: a column of the record that holds it, a simple domain (relation.6, relation.8), a sheet of its own, a nonsimple domain (relation.9-14) [CODD1970], or the sheet of instances, molten (relation.15) [WICKHAM2014].
relation.6	A converter MUST write a property that its schema requires, that is not a kind, and that allows exactly one type, string, number, boolean or null, a simple domain [CODD1970], as a column of its sheet, its field holding the property's value.
relation.7	A converter MUST give a subordinate sheet whose child instances allow exactly one type, string, number, boolean or null, one column, _value, as melting puts data values that have no variable of their own [WICKHAM2014].
relation.8	An attribute maps each entity to a single value or a single tuple of values [CHEN1976], and a property that its schema requires, that is not a kind, and that allows exactly one type, object, and every subschema allOf applies to a location that allows exactly one type, object, apply to every instance they describe [JSON-SCHEMA-07]; so a converter MUST NOT write either as a sheet of its own, but MUST write the members that such a property holds, and those that such a subschema names, as members of the instance that holds them, and take the subschemas that the keywords of either apply as that instance's.
relation.9	A nonsimple domain has relations as elements, what data base terminology calls a repeating group [CODD1970], and independent multivalued facts are kept in separate record types [KENT1983]; so a converter MUST strike from its parent, and write as a subordinate sheet of its own, each subschema applied to child locations that is: a property that allows exactly one type, array; or items, each schema of items given as an array, additionalItems, a pattern of patternProperties, or additionalProperties, whose child instances are the records of the sheet [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
relation.10	A property that allows exactly one type, object, and that its schema does not require, maps its parent to no instance or one, a 1:n mapping [CHEN1976]; so a converter MUST write it as a subordinate sheet, of one record or none.
relation.11	A converter MUST write a subschema that anyOf, oneOf, if, then, else or a dependency applies to the same location, and that allows exactly one type, object, as a subordinate sheet of that location's sheet, where the location is not placed branch by branch (relation.12), whose records are the instances whose annotations it collects: under each branch of anyOf and oneOf an instance is valid against, under if where valid, under then or else as if decides, and under a dependency's schema where the instance holds its key [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
relation.12	A branch applies only to the instances valid against it [JSON-SCHEMA-07], as a property its schema does not require applies only to the instances that hold it, and several types of observational unit in one table are split into one table each, each fact in one place [WICKHAM2014]; so a converter MUST place a child location whose anyOf or oneOf gives its branches, and that allows other than exactly one type, or that relation.8 does not write as its instance's own and whose own schema, apart from its branches, gives it no column and no sheet but those the whole file shares, branch by branch: each instance as the location would be if its schema were the first branch, in the order schema.11 takes them, that the instance is valid against, a property's being taken as not required, the location having no record of its own.
relation.13	A converter MUST write a property that allows exactly one type, array, whose items is one schema, as the sheet of its elements, and an element that is itself an array as a subordinate sheet of its own; and one whose items is an array of schemas as a sheet of one record per array, with a subordinate sheet for each schema of items and for additionalItems, each holding the elements at its positions [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
relation.14	A string that holds FF, a line break or HT, which a field cannot hold [MTSV-DRAFT], and which move to the next page, printing line and position [RFC20], MUST be written as runs of text to the file's one sheet of runs, one type of observational unit in one table [WICKHAM2014], which the schema gives where any location can hold a string: the string split at each FF into pages, each page at each line break, LF or CRLF [MTSV-DRAFT], into lines, and each line at each HT into runs of text, which are the records of the sheet, each with its page, line and position, each a zero-based count, and its value. The runs of a line joined with HT, the lines of a page joined with LF, and the pages joined with FF give the string back, a CRLF reading back as LF, which does not change the structure of MTSV text [MTSV-DRAFT].
relation.15	Molten data, a row, a column and a value, is almost tidy [WICKHAM2014], so a converter MUST write every other value molten, to the file's one sheet of instances, one type of observational unit in one table [WICKHAM2014], which the schema gives where any location is written molten: a property that allows other than exactly one type and is not placed branch by branch (relation.12), or one simple type but is not required; a child location whose subschema allows other than exactly one type and is not placed branch by branch; and the input values themselves, where the root schema allows other than exactly one type, object or array. It holds every instance within the value, the value itself included; where the root schema allows other than exactly one type, object or array, the sheet of the input values is the sheet of instances.
relation.16	A value occurs once in its JSON text [RFC8259], which a schema only describes [JSON-SCHEMA-07], so a converter MUST write each value once. A schema covers a member that its properties names or that a pattern of its patternProperties matches [JSON-SCHEMA-07]; a member is written by its location's own schema where that covers it; else by the first subschema, in the order schema.11 takes them, whose annotations the instance collects and that covers it; else by the first additionalProperties that applies, which takes only the members its schema does not cover [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
relation.17	A record has one column for each domain [CODD1970], so a converter MUST NOT give a sheet a column from an allOf subschema for a property that its location's own schema or an earlier allOf subschema names; where a member is written by an earlier sheet, its field in a later one is empty, its value found by the same key.
key.1	Every record MUST begin with its primary key [CODD1970], fixed variables coming first [WICKHAM2014], all rows of a sheet being distinct [CODD1970].
key.2	A record of the sheet of the input values MUST have one key column, _input value, the zero-based position of its input value in the input (value.2), a primary key none of whose domains is superfluous [CODD1970]; where that sheet is the sheet of instances (relation.15) or a kind's sheet (relation.1), its records have the keys of key.3, the record of an input value leaving _parent and _pointer empty.
key.3	A record of any other sheet but the sheet of runs MUST begin with _input value; then _instance, the zero-based position of its instance among the instances of its input value, the value itself first, counted in the order written (value.5, order.2) [JSON-SCHEMA-07]; then _parent, the _instance of the record whose instance holds its own, which with _input value is a foreign key, possibly into its own relation [CODD1970], as an entity its own attributes cannot identify is identified through the entity that identifies it [CHEN1976]; then _pointer, the JSON Pointer to its instance evaluated from that record's instance, which records where it came from [RFC6901] [WICKHAM2014]. A record whose instance is its location's own, a subschema applying to the same location (relation.2, relation.11), has the _input value and _instance of its location's record, its key augmented by nothing of its own [CODD1970], and leaves _parent and _pointer empty, where it sits being recorded once, in its location's record [WICKHAM2014]. No key holds the access path from the input value to its instance [CODD1970].
key.4	A pointer MUST write '~' in a member name as '~0' and '/' as '~1' [RFC6901].
key.5	A record of the sheet of runs MUST begin with the _input value and _instance of its string, then _page, _line and _position (relation.14), fixed variables coming first [WICKHAM2014], each of the last three a count of the unit FF, LF or HT moves to [RFC20].
order.1	The sheets of a file are ordered [MTSV-DRAFT], and the order of relations is not given [CODD1970]; so a converter MUST write them in the order of the schema's tree: the sheet of the input values first, each sheet directly followed by its subordinate sheets and theirs in turn, in the order schema.11 takes its schema's subschemas; a property or subschema that relation.8 writes as its instance's own gives its sheets in its own place; a kind's sheet, and its subordinate sheets, come in the place of its first reference; and the sheet of instances, where it is not the sheet of the input values, then the sheet of runs, come last.
order.2	The records of a sheet are ordered [MTSV-DRAFT], and the ordering of a relation's rows is immaterial [CODD1970]; so a converter MUST write records in the order the input holds them, and MUST NOT sort them: input values in input order, elements as their array orders them [RFC8259] [JSON-SCHEMA-07], members and instances as written, an instance before the instances within it, and runs of text in the order of their string.
order.3	The ordering of columns is significant, corresponding to the ordering of the domains [CODD1970]; so a converter MUST write the key columns first (key.1), then the columns of the simple domains in the order of the schema's tree (order.1): a location's own properties in the order the schema writes them, the members of a property that relation.8 writes as its instance's own in that property's place, then those an allOf subschema names, in the order of its subschemas.
file.1	A converter MUST write the whole input as one MTSV file [MTSV-DRAFT], which takes the name its caller gives it; what one input is, the module specification of its source says.
file.2	A sheet may hold no records [MTSV-DRAFT], but a relation with no rows holds no value of the input; so a file MUST hold every sheet its schema gives that holds a record, in its place in the order (order.1), and MUST NOT hold one that holds none.
file.3	A converter MUST write each file as a generator [MTSV-DRAFT]: an FF line before every sheet, the first included; a line break after every record; and the file in UTF-8.
sheet.1	Every sheet has a sheet name [MTSV-DRAFT], and the sheet of the input values MUST be named by the title of the root schema [JSON-SCHEMA-07]; where that sheet is the sheet of instances (relation.15) or a kind's sheet (relation.1), it is named as that sheet is (sheet.2, sheet.3).
sheet.2	A kind's sheet MUST be named by the title of its schema, else the last reference token of its $ref [RFC6901].
sheet.3	Every sheet name and field name this specification fixes MUST begin with '_', a mark reserved for them [CSVW-METADATA]: the sheet of instances is named _instances, and the sheet of runs _runs. A name taken or composed from the schema's names that begins with '_' MUST be written with that '_' doubled, as a character with a meaning of its own is escaped by preceding it with another [RFC4180], a label being accommodated by encoding it [CSVW-METADATA] and a percent-encoded '_' not being created [RFC3986].
sheet.4	Every other sheet MUST be named by its parent sheet's name and the parts of its path within the parent's instance, each after '.': the name of each property between them that relation.8 writes as its instance's own, and then: for a property (relation.9, relation.10), the property's name; for a keyword (relation.9, relation.13), the keyword, then the pattern or zero-based position where the keyword holds more than one subschema, and items for the elements of an element that is itself an array [CODD1970]; for a subschema that anyOf, oneOf, if, then, else or a dependency applies (relation.11, relation.12), the keyword, then the subschema's title, else a dependency's key, else its zero-based position where the keyword holds more than one subschema [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
sheet.5	Every sheet MUST have a header, its first record, which contains the name of each field [MTSV-DRAFT], each column labelled with the name of its domain [CODD1970]: the key columns (key.1-5) by their names, _input value, _instance, _parent, _pointer, _page, _line and _position; a simple domain's column by its property's name; a member of a property that relation.8 writes as its instance's own by that property's name, '.', and its own name, the role name qualifying the domain name [CODD1970]; a member an allOf subschema names by its own name; and _type and _value as the Fields define them.
record.1	Each instance, and each run of text, that a relation holds (relation.1-15) MUST be one record of its sheet [CODD1970].
field.1	A field is text [MTSV-DRAFT], and a converter MUST write a string as its characters, a number as its text as written, a boolean as true or false, and null as an empty field [RFC8259] [CSVW].
field.2	In the sheet of instances, the fields after a record's keys MUST be _type, its instance's primitive type, one of the six null, boolean, object, array, number and string [JSON-SCHEMA-07], and _value, its instance's text, empty for null, an object and an array [WICKHAM2014].
field.3	MTSV uses FF, a line break and HT to separate sheets, records and fields, so a field cannot hold them [MTSV-DRAFT]; a converter MUST write a string value that holds any of them as an empty field, its text written as runs of text to the sheet of runs (relation.14).
field.4	A field cannot hold HT, LF, FF or CR [MTSV-DRAFT], and a character that is not a Unicode character, as an unpaired UTF-16 surrogate, is not text [RFC8259] [MTSV-DRAFT], how a generator handles such values being left to it [MTSV-DRAFT]; so a converter MUST NOT write a CR not followed by LF, a character that is not text, or HT, LF, FF or CR in a member name within a pointer, and MUST write the rest of the text.
field.5	A converter MUST report as not carried each thing that field.4 leaves out, by the JSON Pointer to it within the input, whose first reference token is the zero-based position of its input value, as a JSON string [RFC6901].
storage.1	The totality of data in a data bank is a collection of time-varying relations, each subject to insertion, deletion and alteration [CODD1970]; an input's values keep their positions (value.2), so a data bank MUST only insert into an input's stored relations, and MUST NOT delete or alter them.
storage.2	Data about one type of observational unit is often split into files by another variable, the file's name its value [WICKHAM2014]; a data bank MUST store each input apart, named as its module specification names the input (file.1).
storage.3	The sheets each part of an input gives, appended sheet by sheet in the file's order, are the whole input's file (value.2) [MTSV-DRAFT]; a data bank MUST hold, for each input, the relations of its MTSV file for the values stored, the parts inserted in the order the input holds them.
storage.4	A converter writes the whole input's file or none (file.1); a data bank MUST store each value with all its records or with none, and MUST NOT keep anything of a value not wholly stored.
storage.5	A data bank MUST convert the next part of an input from the position equal to the number of values it stores (value.2).
storage.6	Users of a data bank are protected from having to know how its data is organized, its internal representation [CODD1970]; a data bank MUST give what it stores only by communication (communication.1-6).
communication.1	The array representation serves the communication of bulk data between systems [CODD1970], and the output of one tool is the input of another [WICKHAM2014]; a data bank MUST communicate what it stores as MTSV, which the receiving system reads as a parser [MTSV-DRAFT].
communication.2	The verbs of data manipulation are filter, transform, aggregate and sort, each possibly by group, and the output of one tool is the input of another [WICKHAM2014]; a data bank MUST derive what it communicates by filter, and by aggregate only to count an input's values (communication.3), and MUST NOT transform or sort: values as stored (field.1), records in stored order (order.2); any other aggregate is the receiving system's.
communication.3	A user need not know more about a relation than its name and the names of its domains, which the system may offer on request [CODD1970]; a data bank MUST communicate, on request, the names of what it stores as three sheets, each record beginning with its primary key (key.1): _inputs, keyed by _input, then _values, the number of its values stored; _sheets, keyed by _input and _place, then _sheet name; and _fields, keyed by _input, _place and _position, then _field name [MTSV-DRAFT].
communication.4	Filtering subsets observations on a condition, by group [WICKHAM2014], and only the keys are a condition every stored record answers; a data bank MUST communicate, on request, the records it stores of one input, of the values whose positions lie in a given range, its first and its last position both included [RFC7111], each position counted from 0 as key.2 counts it, and of the sheets asked for by their places, or else every sheet, each in its stored sheet, in its place in the order (order.1).
communication.5	Each fact is expressed in only one place [WICKHAM2014]; a data bank MUST communicate what is new as communication.4 does, for the range from the number of values it has communicated to the last value it stores.
communication.6	A data bank MUST keep, with each input's storage, the number of values it has communicated (communication.5).
Fields
Field	Definition
_input value	In every sheet a converter writes, the first column: the zero-based position of the record's input value in the input; the primary key of the sheet of the input values (key.2), with _instance the primary key of every sheet key.3 keys, and with _instance, _page, _line and _position the primary key of the sheet of runs (key.5) [CODD1970].
_instance	In every sheet key.3 or key.5 keys, the second column: the zero-based position of the record's instance among the instances of its input value, the value itself first, in the order written (key.3, key.5) [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
_parent	In every sheet key.3 keys, the third column: the _instance of the record whose instance holds this record's, which with _input value is a foreign key, empty for a record of its location's own instance (key.3) [CODD1970] [CHEN1976].
_pointer	In every sheet key.3 keys, the fourth column: the JSON Pointer to the record's instance, evaluated from its parent's instance, empty where _parent is (key.3, key.4) [RFC6901].
(a property's name)	A column of a relation's sheet, one for each property that is a simple domain, after the key columns, in the order the schema writes them; for a member of a property that relation.8 writes as its instance's own, that property's name, '.', and the member's name, the role name qualifying the domain name (relation.6, relation.8, order.3, sheet.5) [JSON-SCHEMA-07] [CODD1970].
_type	In the sheet of instances, the instance's primitive type: null, boolean, object, array, number or string (field.2) [JSON-SCHEMA-07].
_page	In the sheet of runs, the zero-based count of FF before the run of text: FF moves to the first pre-determined printing line on the next form or page (relation.14) [RFC20].
_line	In the sheet of runs, the zero-based count of line breaks before the run of text within its page: LF moves to the next printing line (relation.14) [RFC20] [MTSV-DRAFT].
_position	In the sheet of runs, the zero-based count of HT before the run of text within its line; in the sheet _fields, the zero-based count of HT before the field within its header: HT moves to the next in a series of predetermined positions along the printing line (relation.14, communication.3) [RFC20].
_value	In the sheet of instances, the instance's text; in the sheet of runs, the run of text; in a sheet whose instances allow one simple type, the instance (relation.7, relation.14, field.2) [MTSV-DRAFT] [WICKHAM2014].
_input	In the sheets _inputs, _sheets and _fields, the first column: an input's name, as its module specification names it (storage.2, communication.3).
_values	In the sheet _inputs, the number of an input's values stored, whose positions a request may name (communication.3, communication.4) [RFC8259].
_place	In the sheets _sheets and _fields, the zero-based place of a sheet among every sheet the schema gives, in the order of order.1, so that it does not change as values are inserted (storage.1, communication.3).
_sheet name	In the sheet _sheets, the sheet's name (communication.3) [MTSV-DRAFT].
_field name	In the sheet _fields, the name of a field, as its sheet's header gives it (communication.3) [MTSV-DRAFT].
